{"kind":"request","text":"Sequence and event identifiers, section 8: dap adopts the protocol (notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md, landed on main at 2fcd50ab1b5b3282621ccdb9ecd2d3aa9dcab396). Order: after the grow-room demo slices of plan 033 (G5a, S2a, G5, demo-room archive, G4) and the journey MVP slices from J3 onward. Take it earlier only as filler when nothing in those orders can move.\n\nScope, as section 8 states it, in one change with no compatibility layer (no deployed history to keep):\n\n1. References. ScopeRef { scope, inc, kind } becomes SequenceRef { scope, genesis }, and FactRef becomes { at: SequenceRef, seq, hash }. The kind leaves references: a handler that tests the kind of a sender reads the genesis of the source scope once and holds it. The scope ID stays in the reference as the address for routing and creation.\n2. Entries. Every entry after the genesis carries genesis (the sha256: digest of the genesis entry) in place of at. The genesis input carries nonce (the 16 random bytes minted today as the incarnation) and no genesis member. The entry hash domain stays artroom-entry-1.\n3. References in a genesis to its own sequence, by the four rules of section 8: { scope, genesis: null } inside a genesis, and in the seeds and messages it sends, kept as sent wherever another entry copies them; a receiver resolves the null from the source of its genesis input or the from of the delivery and records the full reference; a seed that a genesis creates takes as cause the digest of its creator's genesis input, in a new domain artroom-input-1; the genesis digest is computed once, after its sends.\n4. Routing compares the genesis of a send with the receiver's own genesis digest instead of incarnations; wrong-incarnation and incarnation-conflict are renamed to match.\n5. Fact text is the citation did:seq:dap1:\u003cgenesis\u003e/\u003cseq\u003e/\u003chash\u003e. Public refs keep the event hash as one component.\n6. Reads: the HTTP binding of section 5.2 at https://\u003chost\u003e/v1/sequences (sequence document as application/did+json with SeqHttp service ids, one event by position, pages with count 1 to 1000), with the 400/404/403 rules of 5.2. The /v1/scopes/\u003cscope ID\u003e routes stay as the address form. The archive ref stays keyed by scope ID, and head.json carries the genesis digest.\n7. Replay's integrity mode runs levels 0 to 3 of section 4 and writes the report of section 4 exactly (requested, result, reached, reason, anchor, trusts), with anchors as section 4 defines them.\n8. Vectors: vectors/dap1/, vectors/git/ and vectors/git256/, each sequence.json and cases.json with the case list of section 7, produced by a script in a language other than TypeScript that imports no dap code. The tests of replay compare its integrity mode with the dap1 vectors and never regenerate them. dap1 cases include a founding genesis, an act and a preparation with valid signatures, an unsigned delivery, and one changed signature.\n\nSize, from the note: ScopeRef appears in 140 files, FactRef in 88, the incarnation member in 53. Six places need thought: the resolver and routing in the scope package; the creation protocol and its conflict clause; the Directory genesis under rule 3; the chain test of replay; the archive head.json; hand-built reference fixtures. Split into reviewable heads as the work needs; each head lands only after checker approval of that exact head.\n\nIf a rule of section 8 cannot be built as written, stop and ask the planner before choosing another construction; a change to the note goes through review before the code that depends on it.","body":{"conditions":"Done when every head of the change is approved by the checker at its exact head and landed on main, and: (a) no ScopeRef, inc member, wrong-incarnation or incarnation-conflict remains in source, tests or docs; (b) the Directory genesis witness passes: each sibling's scope ID is computed before the genesis is sealed, each sibling records the Directory's full SequenceRef, and a second sequence under the same scope ID creates siblings with different scope IDs; (c) vectors/dap1, vectors/git and vectors/git256 exist with the section 7 case list, produced by the non-TypeScript script, and replay's integrity mode passes the dap1 vectors with exact reports; (d) the HTTP binding of 5.2 is served and tested for the event, page, document and 400/404/403 cases; (e) the gate passes once before each review; (f) no compatibility layer. Ordered after plan 033's grow slices and the journey MVP slices from J3; filler only when those cannot move.","target":"inherit","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}